{"usCongressBioId":"H000629","familyName":"Hindman","givenName":"William","honorificPrefix":"Mr.","unaccentedFamilyName":"Hindman","unaccentedGivenName":"William","birthDate":"1743-04-01","birthCirca":false,"birthDateUnknown":false,"deathDate":"1822-01-19","deathCirca":false,"image":[{"contentUrl":"/bioguide-staging/assets/428b9cc500fd4ac6e4e6bfa07895d29b.jpg","caption":"Maryland Historical Society","name":"H000629_001"}],"profileText":"A Delegate, a Representative, and a Senator from Maryland; born in Dorchester County, Md., April 1, 1743; pursued classical studies; attended the University of Pennsylvania; studied law at the Inns of Court, London, England; returned to the United States, admitted to the bar, and commenced practice in Talbot County, Md.; was secretary of the Talbot (Md.) County committee of observation in 1775 and was designated to execute the resolves of the Continental Congress; sat in the Maryland state convention of 1775 and was named treasurer for the Eastern Shore 1775-1777; member, Maryland state senate, 1777-1784, 1792; Delegate to the Confederation Congress, 1785 and 1786; member of the governor's executive council 1789-1792; elected as a Pro-Administration candidate to the Second Congress to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of Joshua Seney; and reelected to the succeeding Congress; elected as a Federalist to the Fourth and to the succeeding Congress (January 30, 1793-March 3, 1799); member, Maryland state house of delegates, 1799-1800; elected in 1800 as a Federalist to the United States Senate to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of James Lloyd; at expiration of the term was appointed to fill the vacancy caused by the failure of the legislature to elect his successor and served from December 12, 1800, to November 19, 1801; was not a candidate for reelection; engaged in agricultural pursuits on his estate near Wyes Landing; died in Baltimore, Md., January 19, 1822; interment in St. Paul's Burial Ground.","relationship":[]